          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-4 italic">
            Most translations — English, German, Yiddish, Italian, Spanish, Polish, and Esperanto — preserve the bodily image of the neck. 
            French shifts to a different animal metaphor (<em>rétif</em>: a restive horse). Russian and Portuguese abstract the metaphor away entirely into "stubborn" or "obstinate."
            The Hebrew <span class="font-hebrew">עֹרֶף</span> (nape of the neck) is remarkably durable across languages and centuries.
