      <!-- Context -->
      <div class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-6 pb-2 border-b-2 border-cyan-500">Context in Exodus 32</h2>
        <section class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 shadow-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
            Exodus 32:9 falls at a pivotal moment: Moses has been on Sinai for forty days receiving the Torah. 
            Below, the Israelites have fashioned a golden calf. God's statement — <em>"I have seen this people, and behold, they are a stiff-necked people"</em> — 
            comes immediately before the announcement of potential destruction (v. 10), which Moses then intercedes against.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
            The phrase <span class="font-hebrew">קְשֵׁה-עֹרֶף</span> recurs throughout the Torah (Exodus 33:3, 33:5, 34:9; Deuteronomy 9:6, 9:13, 31:27), 
            always as a characterization of the Israelites — sometimes as accusation, sometimes strikingly as Moses's own argument <em>for</em> divine mercy 
            (<em>"if I have found grace in your sight... forgive our iniquity... for it is a stiff-necked people"</em>, Exodus 34:9).
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg p-4">
            <p class="text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-200 text-sm">
              <strong>Note on the metaphor:</strong> The image is of an ox or donkey that refuses to turn its neck when the farmer pulls on the yoke — 
              stubbornly looking forward, refusing redirection. It is physical, visceral, and behavioral: not a question of belief but of responsiveness.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

